WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.920
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.060
Today this is just a quick video to talk about an interesting linguistic phenomenon

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.220
Something that might come up in some of my videos in my future.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.040
And that is the topic of language isolates.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.680
Often when I talk about languages, I talk about the family that language belongs to,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.620
and how it's related to other languages in that family.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.440
For example, Hebrew and Arabic are sister languages of the Semitic Language family

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.440
French and Italian are sister languages of the Romance language family

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.840
But did you know there are some languages that have no family whatsoever?

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.820
Those are called language isolates.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.160
A language isolate, as far as we know, 
has no genetic relationship with any other language.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.920
That means that it does not descend from the same ancestor language as any other language we know of.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.740
They are basically a language family with only one member.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:57.280
Some commonly known language isolates are the Basque language, spoken in parts of France and Spain.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.320
Also the Ainu language which is spoken in Hokkaido &amp; Northern Japan.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.500
Some people say Korean, although that one is disputed.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.500
And some Native American languages as well as some languages on the island of New Guinea.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.080
New Guinea happens to be the most linguistically diverse place on the planet.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.500
If you want to know more about it, check out this video right here.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.140
Why are there language isolates?

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:24.040
Well, one possible reason is that language used to belong to a family that had numerous languages in it,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:28.680
but that 1 particular language became dominant and replaced all of the others.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.140
Or maybe all of the other languages in that family were replaced by a different language.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.040
For example, a language of a conquering army but one of the group refuse to give up

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.500
And their language become a language isolate

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.620
It is possible for a new language isolate to arise,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.840
if that community of people is completely separated, completely isolated from other languages.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.280
That's because children have the ability to spontaneously create new language.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.140
In these modern times, that happens most often with sign languages.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.840
There have been numerous cases,
around the world, of children in deaf schools

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.060
spontaneously creating new sign languages that are completely unrelated to any others in the world.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:12.240
Some linguists say that if you took a group of infants and if you raised them in an environment with no language,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.400
they would spontaneously create 
their own language isolate.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.200
This is just a quick video to introduce that linguistic concept that some people have been talking about

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.540
in response to some of my videos,
in particular the one about Japanese and Korean.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.900
If you have any comments about this topic, leave them down below.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.000
But stay tuned for another feature video coming soon.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.380
Thank you for watching. Have a nice day.

